# mglucy.msg - Lucy West (mglucy.ssl). One entry per line; no braces in comments.

# 100 to 103: look (stranger, known) and examine (waiting, letter posted).
{100}{}{You see a young woman sitting by the door, watching who comes in.}
{101}{}{You see Lucy West.}
{102}{}{She turns a folded piece of paper over and over in her hands. The creases have gone soft.}
{103}{}{Her hands are empty now, and she does not seem to know what to do with them.}

# 110 to 112: floats while she waits. 113: once the letter is on its way.
{110}{}{Two years. He'd be seventeen now.}
{111}{}{Is the caravan in yet?}
{112}{}{Dear Mother, dear Father... no. Start again.}
{113}{}{It's on its way. It's really on its way.}

# 150 to 153: message window.
{150}{}{Lucy West hands you a sealed letter.}
{151}{}{Lucy gives you two bottles of Nuka-Cola.}
{152}{}{Lucy writes the letter out again, seals it, and hands it to you.}
{153}{}{With Lucy West dead, her letter has nobody left to answer to.}

# 200 to 206: greetings by state of the errand.
{200}{}{You came in from outside. You've still got the dust on you. ...Sorry. I'm Lucy. Lucy West. Are you going back out there?}
{201}{}{Oh. Hello again. Have you thought about going back out?}
{202}{}{Did you find the caravan? Did he take it?}
{203}{}{You're back. Did... is it...?}
{204}{}{It's on its way north. I tell myself so every morning. Thank you. Truly.}
{205}{}{Oh. It's you. ...It's all right. I shouldn't have asked a stranger.}
{206}{}{They're saying the caravan master is dead. ...Moira, at Craterside, deals with every caravan that passes. Would you ask her to send it? Please?}

# 210 to 219, 290 to 292: options (213, 219, 291, 292: low Intelligence).
{210}{}{I go where I please. Why do you ask?}
{211}{}{Who are you waiting to hear from?}
{212}{}{Not if I can help it.}
{213}{}{Me go outside. Outside big.}
{214}{}{Not yet.}
{215}{}{I lost your letter.}
{216}{}{I can't get it sent. Take it back.}
{217}{}{What does the letter say?}
{218}{}{It's on its way north.}
{219}{}{Paper go with cow man!}
{290}{}{Goodbye, Lucy.}
{291}{}{Bye.}
{292}{}{Paper gone. Me lose paper.}

# 220 to 228: the errand.
{220}{}{I have a letter for my family, on a homestead a long way north. No word in two years. The caravan outside carries mail, but Harith won't deal with anybody who owes Moriarty, and I owe Moriarty. Would you see it sent?}
{221}{}{Give it here. I'll see it sent.}
{222}{}{What's it worth to you?}
{223}{}{Not my problem.}
{224}{}{Thank you. Thank you. The caravan master camps outside the gate. He'll want thirty dollars from you, I think. Tell him it's for the West place. They know it up north.}
{225}{}{I... forty dollars. It's all I have. It was meant for the postage, but if you'd pay that yourself... take it. Only see that it goes.}
{226}{}{Keep your forty. I'll see it goes.}
{227}{}{Forty will do.}
{228}{}{Here. [She counts it out in small coins.] The caravan master, outside the gate. Tell him it's for the West place. Please. It matters more than I can say.}

# 230 to 234: her family, the letter lost, returned, read out, posted.
{230}{}{My family. Mother, Father, and my brother Ian. He was fifteen when I left, and angry at everything. I write, and nothing comes back. Out there, nothing coming back can mean anything at all.}
{231}{}{Lost it? ...It's all right. I know it by heart. I've written it forty times. [She writes it out again, slowly.] Please don't lose this one.}
{232}{}{Oh. ...No, I understand. It's a great deal to ask of a stranger. [She takes the letter and folds it very small.] Maybe the next one through.}
{233}{}{That I'm well. That the town's strange, but nobody's cruel. That Ian should mind Father and not go wandering after dark. That I'll come home when I have the money. Small things. They're all I have to send.}
{234}{}{It's gone? Truly gone? [She laughs, and then has to wipe her eyes.] I've nothing to give you but these. I was saving them for good news. I think this is it.}

# 240 to 243: low Intelligence.
{240}{}{Yes. Outside. Could you... carry? Carry paper? To the man with the two-headed cows, outside the gate? Paper. To cow man. Please?}
{241}{}{Me carry paper!}
{242}{}{Paper boring.}
{243}{}{Thank you. Don't eat it. Don't... just give it to the cow man.}
